---
import DemoLayout from '../../layouts/DemoLayout.astro';

const numbers: ReadonlyArray<number> = Array.from({ length: 120 }, (_: unknown, index: number): number => index + 1);

const sieveExample: string = `/* Multiples of 2, but not 2 itself */
li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)) { … }

/* With the box ticked, hide every number that has been crossed out */
.sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)) {
    visibility: hidden;
}`;
---
<DemoLayout path="/Demos/Sieve">
    <div slot="demo" class="sieve">
        <div class="sieve-controls">
            <label class="primes-toggle">
                <input type="checkbox" id="primes-only" />
                Show the primes only
            </label>
            <ul class="legend" role="list">
                <li class="legend-2">Multiple of 2</li>
                <li class="legend-3">Multiple of 3</li>
                <li class="legend-5">Multiple of 5</li>
                <li class="legend-7">Multiple of 7</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <ol class="numbers" role="list" aria-label="The numbers 1 to 120">
            {numbers.map(() => <li></li>)}
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Around 2,200 years ago, the Greek mathematician Eratosthenes described a way to find prime numbers without
        dividing anything. Write out the numbers; take the first prime, 2, and cross out all its multiples; move to the
        next number not crossed out, which is the next prime, and cross out its multiples; and carry on. Whatever is
        left is prime.
    </p>
    <p>
        Above, CSS has done the crossing out: each colour marks the multiples of one prime, and the numbers left in plain
        type are the primes from 2 to 113. Tick the box to hide everything that was crossed out.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
        As in <a href="/Demos/FizzBuzz">FizzBuzz</a>, the HTML is a list of empty items numbered by a CSS counter, and
        <code>:nth-child()</code> finds the multiples. Each prime gets one rule, which marks its multiples but skips the
        prime itself:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{sieveExample}</code></pre>
    <p>
        The checkbox is the only state. When it's ticked, <code>:has()</code> lets the whole sieve see that, and hides
        the crossed-out numbers. A number with more than one prime factor, such as 30, is marked by several rules; it
        takes the colour of the last one.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops</h2>
    <p>
        Why only four primes? Every number up to 120 that isn't prime has a prime factor no bigger than its square root,
        and the square root of 120 is just under 11. So crossing out the multiples of 2, 3, 5 and 7 is enough. To go to
        1,000, you'd add rules for 11 up to 31.
    </p>
    <p>
        That's the catch. In a real sieve, the program finds each next prime itself and carries on. Here, someone had to
        know which primes to write rules for. The CSS carries out the algorithm, but it can't extend itself: it computes
        within limits a person set in advance.
    </p>
</DemoLayout>

<style>
    .sieve {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1.25rem;
    }

    .sieve-controls {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 1rem;
    }

    .primes-toggle {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.6rem;
        color: var(--heading);
        font-weight: 500;
        cursor: pointer;
    }

    .primes-toggle input {
        width: 1.25rem;
        height: 1.25rem;
        accent-color: var(--brand-blue);
    }

    .legend {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 0.5rem;
        padding: 0;
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }

    .legend li {
        padding: 0.2rem 0.7rem;
        border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
        corner-shape: round;
        color: #000;
        font-weight: 500;
    }

    .numbers {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(2.75rem, 1fr));
        gap: 0.4rem;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        counter-reset: number;
    }

    .numbers li {
        display: grid;
        place-items: center;
        aspect-ratio: 1;
        border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
        background: var(--surface-alt);
        color: var(--heading);
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
        font-weight: 600;
        counter-increment: number;
    }

    .numbers li::before {
        content: counter(number);
    }

    /* 1 is neither prime nor crossed out; it keeps its place so every other number keeps its position. */
    .numbers li:first-child {
        visibility: hidden;
    }

    .legend-2,
    .numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)) { background: var(--brand-coral); }

    .legend-3,
    .numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)) { background: var(--brand-violet); }

    .legend-5,
    .numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)) { background: var(--brand-blue); }

    .legend-7,
    .numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) { background: var(--brand-teal); }

    .numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)),
    .numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)),
    .numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)),
    .numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) {
        color: #000;
        font-weight: 400;
    }

    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(2n):not(:nth-child(2)),
    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(3n):not(:nth-child(3)),
    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(5n):not(:nth-child(5)),
    .sieve:has(#primes-only:checked) .numbers li:nth-child(7n):not(:nth-child(7)) {
        visibility: hidden;
    }
</style>
